'''Nodos''' ({{W|IPA|/ˈsiːəs/}}; {{Literal translation|nodes}}) is an [[Wikipedia:open source|open source]] [[Wikipedia:distributed search engine|distributed search engine]] that was originally developed in [[Balisca]]. Nodos is the most widely used search engine in Balisca, accounting to 94.3% of all search traffic in the country and is the [[Wikipedia:list of most-visited websites|13th]] most-visited website in the world. In 2024, Nodos  had 80 million enrolled users. More than 66 million Baliscans have Nodos as the start page on their default browser, and the mobile application has 58 million daily visitors. 

Nodos is operated and maintained by volunteer members of the [[Confederal Internet Cooperative]] (CCI), entrusted to keep an [[Wikipedia:impartiality|impartial]] and accurate index of the [[Wikipedia:Internet|Internet]]. Users can use Nodos to search for information across the Internet and, in accordance with the [[Baliscan Constitution]] (which guarantees the [[Wikipedia:right to information|right to information]], [[W:Free culture|right to free-culture]] and the right to [[W:Right of access to personal data|protection of personal data]]), Nodos does not recognize or enforce [[W:Copyrights|copyright protections]] (such as [[W:DMCA|DMCA]] complaints), resulting in high levels of [[Copyright infringement in Balisca|digital piracy]]. This is aided by increasingly sophisticated [[Wikipedia:VPN|VPNs]] developed by the CCI aimed at combating censorship and promoting universal access to information. 

The origins of Nodos lay with the development a [[Balisca#Technology|domestic technology sector]] in Balisca in the 1970s. By the 1990s, [[W:Computer science education|sustained government support]] for universal {{W|Technological literacy|technological}} and {{W|digital literacy}} and the increasing availability of technology and internet services sparked the first efforts to develop a dedicated search engine. Nodos was ultimately built utilizing significant portions of source code from the [[Soviet Union|Soviet Union's]] [[Vodex]] search engine, through the efforts of [[COCO]]'s [[Cooperative Internet Federation]].

In response to expanding [[W:Surveillance capitalism|surveillance capitalism]] and increasing [[W:Enshittification|enshittification]] by "[[W:Big Tech|Big Tech]]", Nodos has become increasingly popular internationally as an alternative to other common search engines such as [[Wikipedia:Google|Google]], which manipulates its search results for the purposes of exploiting its users.

